A caller asking a simple font's metrics to encode a Euro character receives a successful one-byte result that those same metrics decode as comma. This occurs when the accepted font dictionary assigns Euro to code 300 through its Differences array, has no widths or ToUnicode mapping, and is not a subset font. The failure is established from source, not a runtime reproduction. Successful encoding should preserve the requested character; an unusable font code should instead allow the caller to choose a fallback or report an error.

