### Description

When closing the main window on macOS (by clicking the red titlebar close button or issuing a close command), the application terminates, but macOS CrashReporter displays an "unexpectedly quit" crash dialog. No crash trace is written to the app logs.

### Root Cause

In `crates/ui-egui/src/editing.rs`, `PdfCraftApp::guard_quit` checks for unsaved changes. When there are no dirty documents (or discard is confirmed), `guard_quit` allows eframe to drop the root window:

```rust
if !self.allow_quit && self.first_dirty().is_some() {
    ctx.send_viewport_cmd(egui::ViewportCommand::CancelClose);
    self.close_request = Some(CloseRequest::Quit);
} else {
    self.shutdown_recovery();
}
```

On macOS, eframe drops the root `NSWindow` and view while AppKit's run loop is still running. In the subsequent display cycle, AppKit's Touch Bar observation or view hierarchy observers attempt to remove KVO observers from the deallocated view (`-[_NSTouchBarFinderObservation invalidate]`). This raises an uncaught Objective-C exception or `SIGABRT` inside AppKit, which bypasses Rust panic hooks and causes macOS CrashReporter to report an abnormal process termination.

### Proposed Fix

Adopt `craft-quit` from `storytold/craft-libs`:
1. Add `craft-quit` with feature `egui` to workspace dependencies.
2. In `crates/ui-egui/src/lib.rs`, call `craft_quit::on_close_request(ctx)` right after `self.guard_quit(ctx)`.

When close is requested and there are no unsaved changes holding the window open with `CancelClose`, `craft_quit::on_close_request(ctx)` takes over the close, cancels the raw viewport drop via `CancelClose`, and schedules an orderly `-[NSApplication terminate:]` on the main dispatch queue. This allows winit and eframe to run normal exit teardown and exit with code 0 without triggering CrashReporter.

### Problem Description
When opening **Document Properties** (`⌘D` / `File ▸ Properties…`) on a PDF with a long filename (for example, academic downloads, books from the Internet Archive, or legal filings with descriptive titles), the modal dialog stretches horizontally far beyond the screen and viewport boundaries.

In `crates/ui-egui/src/dialogs.rs`, the `File` entry (and read-only metadata fields) in `PropsTab::Description` are drawn using an unwrapped `ui.label`:
```rust
let row = |ui: &mut egui::Ui, k: &str, v: String| {
    ui.label(egui::RichText::new(tl!(k)).color(t.text_muted));
    ui.label(if v.is_empty() { egui::RichText::new("—").color(t.text_faint) } else { egui::RichText::new(v) });
    ui.end_row();
};
```
Because `ui.label(...)` inside `egui::Grid` defaults to a single-line text layout without wrapping, a 200+ character filename forces the grid column to expand to thousands of pixels, blowing out the modal card width past the screen edges and clipping modal buttons and headers.

### Expected Behavior
- Values in Document Properties rows (such as `File`, `Title`, `Author`, etc.) should wrap cleanly within the content column.
- The modal dialog should maintain its designed 640 pt card width and remain centered on the screen regardless of filename length.

PdfCraft 0.8.0 (arm64 .deb) can't start its wgpu renderer on this machine. It falls back to OpenGL (glow) and opens fine there, but it never gets the wgpu renderer.

**Machine:** Raspberry Pi Compute Module 5 Lite (BCM2712, V3D 7.1.10.2), Debian 13 (trixie), Mesa 26.2.2 (`26.2.2-1~bpo13+0~rpt1`), labwc on Wayland.

```
ERROR [main] eframe::native::run: Exiting because of error: WGPU error: Limit 'max_inter_stage_shader_variables' value 16 is better than allowed 15
ERROR [main] pdfcraft: the GPU renderer (wgpu) didn't start: WGPU error: Limit 'max_inter_stage_shader_variables' value 16 is better than allowed 15. Starting with OpenGL instead; set PDFCRAFT_RENDERER=gl to skip wgpu.
```

**Cause:** eframe 0.36's default `WgpuSetupCreateNew::device_descriptor` (egui-wgpu 0.36.2) requests `wgpu::Limits::default()` on every backend except GL, and `downlevel_webgl2_defaults()` on GL, in both cases with `max_texture_dimension_2d: 8192`. It never checks what the adapter offers, so `request_device` fails when the adapter falls short on any single limit. V3D 7.1 falls short on several:

| Limit | requested | V3D 7.1 Vulkan (V3DV) | V3D 7.1 GL |
|---|---|---|---|
| `max_inter_stage_shader_variables` | 16 | 15 (`maxVertexOutputComponents` = 64) | |
| `max_texture_dimension_2d` | 8192 | 7680 | 4096 |

wgpu stops at the first failing limit, so the error only names one of them. egui's own shaders need far less than any of these.

**Fix:** wrap the default descriptor and lower each requested limit to the adapter's where the adapter offers less:

```rust
if let eframe::egui_wgpu::WgpuSetup::CreateNew(setup) = &mut native_options.wgpu_options.wgpu_setup {
    let egui_descriptor = std::sync::Arc::clone(&setup.device_descriptor);
    setup.device_descriptor = std::sync::Arc::new(move |adapter| {
        let mut descriptor = egui_descriptor(adapter);
        descriptor.required_limits = descriptor.required_limits.or_worse_values_from(&adapter.limits());
        descriptor
    });
}
```

